    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genghis-khan.jpg" alt="Genghis Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumGenghisPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kazakh Khanate grew out of the political world of the White Horde, the eastern wing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, whose khans descended from Jochi, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. In the mid-fifteenth century most of the steppe east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-river">Ural River</a> was ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-khayr-khan">Abu'l-Khayr Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaybanids">Shibanid</a>. Two sultans of the rival line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urus-khan">Urus Khan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerei-khan">Kerei</a> and his kinsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janibek-khan">Janibek</a>, broke with him, and around 1465, the date given by the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-haidar-dughlat">Muhammad Haidar Dughlat</a>, they led their followers south-east into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esen-buqa-ii">Esen Buqa Khan</a> allowed them to settle in the western Chu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taraz">Talas</a> valleys. Their people became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a>, a Turkic word meaning free or independent wanderers, a name earlier applied to men who had broken away from their own tribe or ruler. Many clans left Abu'l-Khayr to join them, and after his death in 1468 or 1469 the Uzbek confederation fell apart. Most of the Uzbek nomads later followed Abu'l-Khayr's grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani</a> south into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, and the Kazakhs became masters of most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dasht-i-qipchaq">Dasht-i Qipchaq</a>, the great steppe north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>. Kerei is usually counted as the first khan and was followed by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burunduk-khan">Burunduk</a>, while Janibek's son Qasim became the dominant figure by the early sixteenth century and is regarded as the khanate's first great ruler.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/turkistan-city"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turkistan-city.jpg" alt="Turkistan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/turkistan-city">Turkistan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1914_map_of_Asia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> were mounted pastoralists who moved their herds of sheep, horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> between summer pastures in the north and winter quarters in the south, along routes fixed by custom and clan. They lived in felt yurts, and wealth was counted in livestock; mare's milk (<em>kumis</em>) and mutton were staples. Some farming was practised in the river valleys, and the oasis towns of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sauran">Sauran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sighnaq">Sygnak</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayram">Sayram</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkistan-city">Turkistan</a>, gave the khans access to crafts, markets and Muslim scholarship. Trade linked the steppe with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>, where the nomads sold horses, sheep, hides and wool for cloth, grain and metal goods, and from the eighteenth century with Russian trading posts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orenburg">Orenburg</a>. Society was divided into the 'white bone' (<em>aq suyek</em>), made up of the Genghisid sultans (<em>tore</em>) and the holy lineages of <em>qoja</em>, and the 'black bone' commoners, organised in genealogical clans and tribes whose ancestry every adult was expected to know. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread gradually among the nomads, shaped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-yasawi">Ahmad Yasawi</a>, while older customs persisted alongside it. Oral poetry was the main literary form: <em>zhyrau</em> poet-advisers, from the semi-legendary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asan-kaigy">Asan Kaigy</a> in the fifteenth century to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhar-zhyrau">Bukhar Zhyrau</a> in the eighteenth, and singers of heroic epics preserved history, law and genealogy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-haidar-dughlat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-haidar-dughlat.jpg" alt="Muhammad Haidar Dughlat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-haidar-dughlat">Muhammad Haidar Dughlat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_coin_of_Haidar_Dughlat_in_name_of_Humayun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rani nurmai, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakh</a> armies were tribal cavalry led by sultans and by renowned warriors (<em>batyr</em>), fighting with bows, lances and sabres and, from the seventeenth century, increasingly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlocks</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasym-khan">Qasim Khan</a> (r. c. 1511–1521) they were feared across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-haidar-dughlat">Muhammad Haidar Dughlat</a> credits him with a following of about a million people. The khans fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaybanids">Shaybanids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> for the towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogais">Nogais</a> to the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghuls</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a> to the east. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haqnazar-khan">Haqnazar Khan</a> (r. c. 1538–1580) restored Kazakh power after a period of defeats, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tauekel-khan">Tauekel Khan</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkistan-city">Turkistan</a> and other towns from Bukhara in 1598, gains held under his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esim-khan">Esim Khan</a>. From the 1630s the main threat came from the Dzungar Khanate. In 1643 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir-khan">Jahangir Khan</a>, with a small force of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeers</a> and help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalangtush-bahadur">Yalangtush Bahadur of Samarkand</a>, is said to have checked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdeni-batur">Erdeni Batur</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-orbulaq">Battle of Orbulaq</a> in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semirechye">Zhetysu</a>. Dzungar pressure grew under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galdan-boshugtu-khan">Galdan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsewang-rabtan">Tsewang Rabtan</a> and culminated in the invasion of 1723. The three juzes then joined forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abulkhair-khan">Abulkhair Khan</a> and batyrs such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabanbay-batyr">Kabanbai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogenbai-batyr">Bogenbai</a>, winning victories at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bulanty">Bulanty</a> around 1727–1728 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-anyrakai">Anyrakai</a> around 1729–1730, but their alliance broke apart soon afterwards over rivalry for leadership.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/esim-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/esim-khan.jpg" alt="Esim Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/esim-khan">Esim Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Kazakh_Khanate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">naturalearthdata.com, offered to the Public Domain per Terms of Use, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The khanate was a confederation rather than a centralised state. Only descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> could become khan or sultan, and a new khan was raised on a white felt by the assembled nobles; in practice several khans often reigned at once over different groups, and a khan's authority rested on personal prestige, success in war and the loyalty of clan leaders. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> were grouped into three hordes, or <em>juz</em>: the Senior Juz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semirechye">Zhetysu</a>, the Middle Juz in the central and northern steppe and the Junior Juz in the west. Their origins are uncertain, but they became political units in their own right during the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. Justice lay with the <em>biy</em>, elders respected for their eloquence and knowledge of customary law (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adat">adat</a></em>). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esim-khan">Esim Khan</a> (r. 1598–1628) is remembered for a body of rules called 'the old road of Esim Khan', and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tauke-khan">Tauke Khan</a> (r. c. 1680–1715) for the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhety-zhargy">Zhety Zhargy</a></em>, the 'Seven Charters', a code traditionally attributed to him and to the leading biys of the three juzes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tole-bi">Tole Bi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazybek-bi">Kazybek Bi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aiteke-bi">Aiteke Bi</a>. It regulated blood money (<em>kun</em>), disputes over land and livestock, marriage and the defence of the realm. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkistan-city">Turkistan</a>, with the mausoleum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-yasawi">Ahmad Yasawi</a> built on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, served as a seat of the khans and their burial place.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abylai-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abylai-khan.jpg" alt="Abylai Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abylai-khan">Abylai Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2000_Stamp_of_Kazakhstan_-_Abylai_Khan_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kazpost, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kazakh Khanate gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> their name, their territorial frame and much of their political memory. The division into three juzes, the role of the biys as mediators and the genealogical clan system survived the khanate and remained important into the twentieth century, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhety-zhargy">Zhety Zhargy</a> became the model of Kazakh customary law. By bringing much of the steppe between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> under Kazakh khans, the khanate formed the space on which the borders of the Kazakh Soviet Socialist Republic, and in 1991 of independent Kazakhstan, were later drawn. Its long struggle with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a>, above all the 'Years of the Great Disaster' (<em>Aqtaban shubyryndy</em>) after 1723, became central to Kazakh national history, as did <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abylai-khan">Abylai Khan</a> and the batyrs who fought beside him. Kazakh oral literature, from the zhyrau poets to the epics of the batyrs, preserved this memory and laid the foundations of the modern literary language. The khanate's turn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the eighteenth century also had wider consequences: it opened the steppe to Russian forts, officials and settlers and prepared the way for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-conquest-of-central-asia">Russian conquest of Central Asia</a> in the nineteenth century. In modern Kazakhstan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerei-khan">Kerei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janibek-khan">Janibek</a> are commemorated by monuments, and the 550th anniversary of the khanate's foundation was marked with state celebrations in 2015.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abulkhair-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abulkhair-khan.jpg" alt="Abulkhair Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abulkhair-khan">Abulkhair Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abulkhair_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Castle, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tauke-khan">Tauke Khan</a>'s death around 1715 the khanate lost its central leadership, and the three juzes went their separate ways under their own khans. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> invasion of 1723 drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> from their eastern pastures and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> towns, and although they recovered some ground, fear of the Dzungars and pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmyks">Kalmyks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkirs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> led them to seek protection. In 1731 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abulkhair-khan">Abulkhair Khan</a> of the Junior Juz swore allegiance to Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-of-russia">Anna of Russia</a>, and parts of the Middle Juz followed; the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orenburg">Orenburg</a> was founded in 1743 as a base on the steppe frontier. After the Qing destroyed the Dzungars in the 1750s, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abylai-khan">Abylai Khan</a> of the Middle Juz balanced between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the Qing and was proclaimed khan of all three juzes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkistan-city">Turkistan</a> in 1771, but his authority did not outlast his death in 1781. Russia then dismantled the khanship: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regulations-on-the-siberian-kirgiz">Regulations of 1822</a> drafted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-speransky">Mikhail Speransky</a> abolished the office in the Middle Juz, the khan of the Junior Juz was removed in 1824, and the steppe was divided into districts under Russian officials. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenesary-kasymov">Kenesary Kasymov</a>, a grandson of Abylai, led a revolt from 1837, was proclaimed khan in 1841 and was killed fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyz-people">Kyrgyz</a> in 1847. The Senior Juz passed under Russian rule during the advance into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semirechye">Zhetysu</a> in the following decades.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kazakh Khanate united the nomads of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakh</a> steppe under Genghisid khans for nearly four centuries, giving the Kazakhs their name, their division into three juzes and their customary law, while its wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> and turn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> shaped the territory and memory of modern Kazakhstan.</em></p>

